1
00:00:09,236 --> 00:00:13,061
Hey, hey, Akane, what's "dos...koi"?

2
00:00:13,195 --> 00:00:15,445
It's a DoS attack.

3
00:00:15,778 --> 00:00:16,912
(Sumo wrestler) DOSUKOI!

4
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:23,860
Well, in terms of bringing something down with brute force,
that's not entirely off the mark.

5
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:28,669
Thank you for that... novel contribution.

6
00:00:29,136 --> 00:00:31,003
That's not what I meant!

7
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:32,140
This morning!

8
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:36,920
I got a call about dos...koi and IPs and whatnot!

9
00:00:37,428 --> 00:00:40,161
From some "Yoshimitsu" something or other.

10
00:00:40,262 --> 00:00:42,978
From Professor Asagiri Yoshimitsu!?

11
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:45,080
The security expert!?

12
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:46,569
...Probably.

13
00:00:46,887 --> 00:00:49,869
I thought it was a prank call, so I hung up.

14
00:00:51,369 --> 00:00:53,640
Ah, pardon the sudden call.

15
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:58,578
This is Hosono from Kyoki Railway.

16
00:00:58,977 --> 00:01:00,643
Grrrrrrr...

17
00:01:00,876 --> 00:01:04,040
Whoa, he's really angry!

18
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:07,100
What!? Even external websites!?

19
00:01:07,825 --> 00:01:10,640
Yes, it really does seem to be an indiscriminate attack.

20
00:01:11,469 --> 00:01:15,136
Hmm, could it actually be a simple mechanism?

21
00:01:15,469 --> 00:01:16,519
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

22
00:01:16,544 --> 00:01:17,552
Hm?

23
00:01:17,836 --> 00:01:19,319
Emergency signal!?

24
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:22,460
Where!?

25
00:01:22,461 --> 00:01:24,476
No, that's just a keyboard sound effect.

26
00:01:24,577 --> 00:01:26,443
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

27
00:01:26,627 --> 00:01:28,143
(Managura) Oh, that's confusing.

28
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:31,120
I was testing my custom OS, and...

29
00:01:32,595 --> 00:01:35,300
Playing around at a time like-- Hm?

30
00:01:35,628 --> 00:01:37,345
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

31
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:38,540
(Akane) F5?

32
00:01:38,644 --> 00:01:40,840
(Lt. Commander) F5! The F5 key!

33
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:44,078
(Lt. Commander) The F5 key is being pressed repeatedly on its own!?

34
00:01:44,411 --> 00:01:46,760
So this is an "F5 attack"?

35
00:01:48,103 --> 00:01:49,253
If it's the F5 key--

36
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:51,703
The operations rescheduling module!

37
00:01:52,837 --> 00:01:54,337
That's it!

38
00:01:54,640 --> 00:02:00,120
So the F5 attack triggered the operations rescheduling module,

39
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:06,620
which triggered its chronic deadlock bug, escalating into a system failure.
Is that right?

40
00:02:08,306 --> 00:02:12,528
However, to infect my custom OS... Quite the skilled adversary.

41
00:02:14,394 --> 00:02:16,878
Regardless of the OS--

42
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:18,700
Could it be!?

43
00:02:18,828 --> 00:02:19,845
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

44
00:02:20,028 --> 00:02:21,762
(Keyboard sound stops)

45
00:02:22,875 --> 00:02:25,094
It stopped when I unplugged the keyboard.

46
00:02:25,378 --> 00:02:26,675
(Managura) No way!

47
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:28,690
Hey, everyone!

48
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:31,136
Unplug your keyboards!

49
00:02:39,695 --> 00:02:41,595
Ohh!

50
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:44,940
It was never a virus to begin with.

51
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:49,428
But didn't that guy remove
a virus this morning?

52
00:02:49,678 --> 00:02:51,545
(Phone vibration)

53
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:52,950
This is Yumesaki.

54
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:56,360
Is there anything suspicious connected to Terminal 13?

55
00:02:57,053 --> 00:02:59,560
Terminal 13...

56
00:03:00,053 --> 00:03:01,203
(Uyagawa) Heave-ho...

57
00:03:01,303 --> 00:03:02,370
Ah!

58
00:03:07,929 --> 00:03:08,929
Ow ow ow ow!

59
00:03:09,119 --> 00:03:10,119
Hey!

60
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:11,260
What are you doing!?

61
00:03:12,010 --> 00:03:13,520
Clever of you.

62
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:17,369
If you repeatedly press F5 on multiple terminals,

63
00:03:17,919 --> 00:03:20,940
it causes a deadlock in the train operations management system.

64
00:03:22,653 --> 00:03:23,740
Right?

65
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:26,761
You got on the Chief Dispatcher's bad side during the
Train Operations Management System renewal project,

66
00:03:26,945 --> 00:03:30,328
didn't you, Mr. Uyagawa?

67
00:03:30,827 --> 00:03:32,543
Furthermore,

68
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:36,293
a small computer was hidden inside the keyboard,

69
00:03:36,493 --> 00:03:41,427
programmed to trigger the F5 barrage only when
the Scroll Lock key was turned on.

70
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:44,220
Right?

71
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:49,040
The person who could remove a virus just by toggling Scroll Lock--Mr. Uyagawa?

72
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:56,980
These keyboards were purchased from your company,
Kyotetsu IT Solutions.

73
00:03:58,160 --> 00:03:59,160
Right?

74
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:01,520
A supply chain attack, in a way!

75
00:04:03,545 --> 00:04:04,545
Mr. Uyagawa?

76
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:08,380
Ha ha ha...

77
00:04:08,430 --> 00:04:10,160
Go ask the keyboard supplier!

78
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:11,660
It wasn't me!

79
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:12,920
Is that so?

80
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:15,645
From what we heard about the Agaho incident,

81
00:04:15,895 --> 00:04:19,595
it seems you knew everything from the start.

82
00:04:19,762 --> 00:04:22,062
Yeah, I knew about the keyboard defect.

83
00:04:22,690 --> 00:04:26,169
Then why, when the operations management system went down,

84
00:04:26,387 --> 00:04:30,503
didn't you point out the keyboard anomaly?

85
00:04:30,610 --> 00:04:36,560
No-- rather, why didn't you propose replacing the keyboards
much sooner?

86
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:40,480
Well, Mr. Uyagawa, you ninja washout?

87
00:04:41,790 --> 00:04:44,980
But Scroll Lock doesn't turn on by itself!

88
00:04:47,510 --> 00:04:50,680
Indeed, that puzzled me too.

89
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:55,280
The F5 barrage wouldn't trigger unless someone turned it on.

90
00:04:56,050 --> 00:04:58,820
(Uyagawa) It was the PR Department's Ultimate Bug Trigger!

91
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:04,600
True, at least the incidents in the PR Division were caused that way.

92
00:05:05,662 --> 00:05:06,828
However--

93
00:05:07,290 --> 00:05:11,828
Mr. Uyagawa, this USB drive you were carrying--

94
00:05:11,978 --> 00:05:15,562
something curious happens when you plug it in.

95
00:05:16,769 --> 00:05:21,145
Despite being a USB drive, it behaves like a keyboard,

96
00:05:21,278 --> 00:05:25,445
automatically inputting the Scroll Lock key
and turning Scroll Lock on.

97
00:05:25,612 --> 00:05:27,595
Heh heh heh.

98
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:29,680
And that's not all.

99
00:05:30,516 --> 00:05:33,154
It opens "Run",

100
00:05:33,316 --> 00:05:36,216
and using a combination of PowerShell and psexec,

101
00:05:36,415 --> 00:05:41,884
it turns on Scroll Lock on every computer in the network.

102
00:05:44,130 --> 00:05:48,280
And it's thoughtfully designed to execute every few minutes.

103
00:05:48,610 --> 00:05:53,100
Ha, ha ha ha... Quite an elaborate method, isn't it?

104
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:56,920
But I saw something suspicious plugged in and removed it.

105
00:05:57,260 --> 00:05:58,260
That's all.

106
00:06:00,012 --> 00:06:02,595
Oh? Even now, you maintain that?

107
00:06:03,344 --> 00:06:04,778
However,

108
00:06:04,803 --> 00:06:09,862
the moment you plugged in that USB drive was caught on camera.

109
00:06:09,887 --> 00:06:12,158
By this fine fellow.

110
00:06:13,261 --> 00:06:14,628
Bzzz.

111
00:06:14,712 --> 00:06:17,478
The multi-purpose self-propelled Pachira unit.

112
00:06:18,795 --> 00:06:19,862
Huh!?

113
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:27,800
What's more, the TV camera also caught you from another angle.

114
00:06:29,025 --> 00:06:34,680
And you know, psexec does leave traces in the logs, Mr. Uyagawa.

115
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:38,080
That's right. There's no escape now.

116
00:06:39,670 --> 00:06:44,761
The culprit is you, Uyagawa Yasu.

117
00:06:44,962 --> 00:06:48,062
Q.E.D.

118
00:06:56,870 --> 00:06:59,770
I get off work in 30 minutes,

119
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:02,853
so confess already, would you?

120
00:07:03,403 --> 00:07:05,687
Damn it! Damn it!

121
00:07:06,453 --> 00:07:09,803
Yeah, that attitude is what pisses me off!

122
00:07:10,003 --> 00:07:12,600
So what if you're the parent company!? You're all garbage!

123
00:07:13,355 --> 00:07:16,000
After everything I've done for you, every last one of you!

124
00:07:17,045 --> 00:07:18,580
That's why I did it.

125
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:22,125
(Yumesaki) Uyagawa, I understand how you feel, but...

126
00:07:22,149 --> 00:07:25,950
(Akane) Well, they really are garbage, but... what else is new?

127
00:07:25,974 --> 00:07:29,320
...It's because of your garbage projects
that we got demoted!

128
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:32,361
Well, anticipating something like this,

129
00:07:32,412 --> 00:07:34,778
I'd been quietly setting this up for three years.

130
00:07:35,428 --> 00:07:37,595
(Katsuragi) What did you say!?

131
00:07:39,150 --> 00:07:41,800
Do you have any idea how much damage you've caused!?

132
00:07:42,740 --> 00:07:44,420
Ha ha ha ha ha!

133
00:07:44,470 --> 00:07:45,940
You brought this on yourselves!

134
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:51,080
Your behavior made everyone start covering things up.

135
00:07:51,915 --> 00:07:53,903
That's why the response was delayed!

136
00:07:54,019 --> 00:07:57,400
Congratulations! You're an "accomplice" of the hacker!

137
00:07:57,940 --> 00:07:58,940
Ha ha ha ha!

138
00:07:59,060 --> 00:08:00,160
Show me!

139
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:03,420
That "railroader's pride" of yours!

140
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:06,000
Ha ha ha ha ha ha ha!

141
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:17,760
Yesterday, I cleaned up my room.

142
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:21,000
Oh, that's rare.

143
00:08:22,523 --> 00:08:23,523
Eek!

144
00:08:29,870 --> 00:08:31,370
My ears!

145
00:08:55,820 --> 00:08:58,170
Ah! A U-F-O!

146
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,320
Oh, you're right!

147
00:09:03,250 --> 00:09:05,340
A "U-FO"...nium (euphonium)!?

148
00:09:10,753 --> 00:09:23,086
(Performance)

149
00:09:23,761 --> 00:09:26,061
Wait, this is a tuba!

150
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:27,600
I thought it was heavy.

151
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:29,275
Well then!

152
00:09:29,359 --> 00:09:30,425
Bye-bye!

153
00:09:30,780 --> 00:09:31,780
Oh no!

154
00:09:32,330 --> 00:09:34,880
The tsukkomi-bait vulnerability got me...

155
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:41,569
I was so close... to leaving on time...

156
00:09:41,670 --> 00:09:45,487
There's nothing we can "doe" about it! Gotta protect that clock-out time!

157
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:48,245
Raaaaahh!

158
00:09:52,762 --> 00:09:55,012
Heh heh heh heh.

159
00:09:57,445 --> 00:09:59,512
(Uyagawa) Skippity-skip-skip-skip~

160
00:10:00,153 --> 00:10:01,620
Whee-hee!

161
00:10:01,937 --> 00:10:03,887
Ugh, no way... I can't...

162
00:10:07,770 --> 00:10:10,253
(Uyagawa) Adios!

163
00:10:10,870 --> 00:10:12,820
Bye-bye~

164
00:10:12,895 --> 00:10:14,078
(Akane) Ahhh...

165
00:10:14,103 --> 00:10:15,178
(Uyagawa) Ha ha ha ha!

166
00:10:15,228 --> 00:10:16,544
(Siren sound)
(Akane) Huh?

167
00:10:16,828 --> 00:10:19,445
Akaneeee!

168
00:10:19,528 --> 00:10:20,594
Wha!?

169
00:10:21,745 --> 00:10:23,778
What is... going on?

170
00:10:25,128 --> 00:10:28,095
I thought about what I have to offer.

171
00:10:28,862 --> 00:10:30,628
Connections.

172
00:10:31,928 --> 00:10:33,362
Kyaaaa!

173
00:10:34,728 --> 00:10:36,680
Emergency vehicle coming through!

174
00:10:38,187 --> 00:10:42,636
(Singing) Momotaro-san, Momotaro-san...

175
00:10:57,095 --> 00:10:59,078
We're out of gas...!

176
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:06,260
Kyotetsu Bus, please help!

177
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:08,720
You got it! Leave it to me!

178
00:11:16,500 --> 00:11:21,760
Wait for meee! Leaving on tiiiime!

179
00:11:22,728 --> 00:11:24,778
...Exercise... no good.

180
00:11:27,595 --> 00:11:29,812
Akaneeee!

181
00:11:29,995 --> 00:11:34,912
When you're bad at something, just rely on someone who's good at it!

182
00:11:36,950 --> 00:11:38,016
Oh, right.

183
00:11:38,041 --> 00:11:39,487
(Phone ringing)

184
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:43,520
Grr!

185
00:11:47,437 --> 00:11:48,853
Grr!!

186
00:11:51,978 --> 00:11:52,978
Grr...

187
00:11:54,378 --> 00:11:56,112
Leave it to me, Akane!

188
00:12:03,087 --> 00:12:05,470
Raaaaaahhh!

189
00:12:07,537 --> 00:12:10,020
Raaaaaahhh!

190
00:12:11,953 --> 00:12:14,170
Raaaaaahhh!

191
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:15,560
Go!

192
00:12:17,111 --> 00:12:20,261
Raaaaaahhhhhh!
(PA) The Tokyo-bound train is now departing.

193
00:12:20,462 --> 00:12:23,328
(PA) The doors are closing. Please stand clear.

194
00:12:25,012 --> 00:12:26,312
Huh!?

195
00:12:28,966 --> 00:12:30,886
Oh no!

196
00:12:31,153 --> 00:12:32,370
Bye-bye~

197
00:12:32,537 --> 00:12:35,403
Stop right theeeere!

198
00:12:36,803 --> 00:12:37,869
Huh?

199
00:12:40,228 --> 00:12:41,980
Railway police!

200
00:12:43,665 --> 00:12:47,711
Under arrest for Obstruction of Business by Damaging a Computer—

201
00:12:47,878 --> 00:12:49,545
You're under arrest!

202
00:12:50,015 --> 00:12:51,756
Ow! Ow ow ow ow ow!

203
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:54,080
4:59 PM. Arrested!

204
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:58,000
I've always wanted to say that!

205
00:12:58,024 --> 00:13:00,150
Akane, did you hear!?

206
00:13:00,174 --> 00:13:03,833
(Phone) Like, a guy in a full bodysuit was the culprit? Way too obvious.

207
00:13:03,874 --> 00:13:05,477
(Phone) First time I've seen one in real life.

208
00:13:06,486 --> 00:13:11,081
Well, in any case, case closed.

209
00:13:16,495 --> 00:13:20,928
Our obsession with the ideal of "zero security incidents"

210
00:13:21,162 --> 00:13:26,345
left our emergency response capabilities underdeveloped. We cannot deny that.

211
00:13:28,220 --> 00:13:33,553
We will promptly review our security response and training systems,

212
00:13:33,870 --> 00:13:38,887
and establish a cross-departmental dedicated security team.

213
00:13:43,927 --> 00:13:47,682
We deeply apologize to our passengers

214
00:13:47,707 --> 00:13:51,200
and all parties involved

215
00:13:51,225 --> 00:13:55,271
for the inconvenience and concern we have caused.

216
00:13:56,750 --> 00:13:58,470
We are truly sorry.

217
00:14:09,435 --> 00:14:10,490
Regarding this incident,

218
00:14:20,150 --> 00:14:24,070
their prompt disclosure of facts and investigation results is commendable.

219
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:31,410
So just deploying security products isn't enough to be safe.

220
00:14:33,190 --> 00:14:37,910
Every individual--management and employees alike--must have a sense of ownership.

221
00:14:39,486 --> 00:14:43,225
Assuming attacks will come, whether internal or external,

222
00:14:43,249 --> 00:14:47,878
establishing incident response systems and continuously reviewing them,

223
00:14:48,095 --> 00:14:52,462
in a way that fits the organization's culture--that's what's needed.

224
00:14:54,228 --> 00:14:57,070
And stop hanging up phone calls!

225
00:15:01,070 --> 00:15:05,053
Cyberattacks on critical infrastructure are expected to increase.

226
00:15:05,237 --> 00:15:10,320
All eyes are on Kyoki Railway's future initiatives.

227
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:20,570
So Katsuragi got exiled. Serves him right.

228
00:15:22,670 --> 00:15:25,293
Still, it wasn't just his problem alone.

229
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:27,936
In terms of a sense of ownership...

230
00:15:29,130 --> 00:15:30,130
Yeah.

231
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:35,890
I never thought railway and information security had so much in common.

232
00:15:37,910 --> 00:15:40,490
Hey, you've been thinking about something, haven't you?

233
00:15:42,095 --> 00:15:43,262
Yes.

234
00:15:43,310 --> 00:15:47,816
The System Division was originally just parked under PR for budget reasons,

235
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:52,133
but I'm thinking we should make better use of our position in PR.

236
00:16:12,519 --> 00:16:13,720
CSIRT?

237
00:16:13,890 --> 00:16:18,300
Yes, a cross-departmental team that handles security incidents.

238
00:16:19,700 --> 00:16:22,040
Oh, what they announced at the press conference.

239
00:16:23,180 --> 00:16:25,120
We still have a long way to go, though.

240
00:16:26,230 --> 00:16:30,560
We need to earn trust so that reports and communication flow to us naturally.

241
00:16:32,660 --> 00:16:36,441
Come to think of it, the key to solving this case

242
00:16:36,465 --> 00:16:38,300
was Agaho's tip-off.

243
00:16:39,140 --> 00:16:40,140
Well...

244
00:16:42,519 --> 00:16:45,041
In that sense, Agaho,

245
00:16:45,398 --> 00:16:48,252
thank you as always.

246
00:16:57,393 --> 00:16:59,560
Nice! Keep up the good work!

247
00:16:59,726 --> 00:17:01,115
And so, this time,

248
00:17:01,139 --> 00:17:04,726
we've decided to hire an ethical hacker for the company.

249
00:17:04,993 --> 00:17:06,393
What!?

250
00:17:06,460 --> 00:17:08,593
(Ethical Hacker) Ethical~!

251
00:17:08,726 --> 00:17:12,140
Ha ha... It seems we still have a long road ahead...
